      "description": "The SPF record ends in '?all' (Neutral). RFC 7208 section 2.6.2 defines Neutral as the domain explicitly not asserting whether a sending server is authorised, and section 8.2 requires receivers to treat it exactly like the result for a domain with no SPF policy. Servers the record lists still pass; for every other server, SPF gives the same answer as having no SPF policy. DMARC does not enforce a policy here, so nothing stops forged mail. That exposure is reported as dmarc-policy-none. This finding is the same weakness seen from the SPF side: count the two as one.",
      "impact": "For senders the record does not list, SPF gives receivers nothing to act on. Whether forged mail is stopped then rests on DMARC, with DKIM, and on each receiver's own policy.",
      "remediation": "Change '?all' to '-all' once every legitimate sender is listed, or to '~all' while you are still confirming that.",
      "references": [
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-5.1"
      ],
      "taxonomy": {
        "cwe": [
          "CWE-290"
